==Welsh==

===Etymology===
{{inh|cy|cel-bry-pro|*huɨl}}, early borrowing from {{der|cy|ang|seġl}} or {{der|cy|non|segl}}, both from {{der|cy|gem-pro|*seglą|t=sail}}. Other meanings apparently derive from this, via the sense of ''successful progress'', perhaps influenced by {{m|cy|gŵyl||holiday, feast}}.<ref>{{R:cy:GPC}}</ref> Cognate with {{cog|br|gouel||sail; feast}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|cy|/huːɨ̯l/|a=cy-N}}
* {{IPA|cy|/hʊi̯l/|a=cy-S}}
* {{rhymes|cy|uːɨ̯l|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{cy-noun|f|hwyliau}}

# [[sail]]
#: {{ux|cy|Lledwch yr '''hwyliau'''!|Unfurl the '''sails'''!}}
# [[mood]]
#: {{ux|cy|Dyw hi ddim mewn '''hwyliau''' da heddiw.|She's not in a good '''mood''' today.}}
# [[journey]], [[route]], [[progress]], [[success]]
#: {{ux|cy|Sut '''hwyl''' gest ti?|How did you '''get on'''? (lit. What kind of success did you have?)}}
#: {{ux|cy|Pob '''hwyl''' i chi!|All '''the best'''! (lit. Every success to you!)}}
# [[fun]]
#: {{ux|cy|Mae'n un sy'n llawn '''hwyl'''.|He's a guy / She's a girl that's full of '''fun'''.}}
# [[fervour]], ecstatic inspiration, especially as exhibited by [[Nonconformist]] [[preacher]]s
#: {{ux|cy|Mae'r pregethwr yn yr '''hwyl'''.|The preacher is at the '''high point of his sermon'''.}}

